{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"nl -ba ../native-source/replication.go | sed -n '135,198p;440,532p'; nl -ba ../native-source/raft_test.go | sed -n '1690,1820p'; nl -ba ../native-source/transport.go | sed -n '1,125p'; nl -ba ../native-source/inmem_transport.go | sed -n '115,230p'; nl -ba ../native-source/README.md | sed -n '60,110p'\"","aggregated_output":"   135\t// replicate is a long running routine that replicates log entries to a single\n   136\t// follower.\n   137\tfunc (r *Raft) replicate(s *followerReplication) {\n   138\t\t// Start an async heartbeating routing\n   139\t\tstopHeartbeat := make(chan struct{})\n   140\t\tdefer close(stopHeartbeat)\n   141\t\tr.goFunc(func() { r.heartbeat(s, stopHeartbeat) })\n   142\t\n   143\tRPC:\n   144\t\tshouldStop := false\n   145\t\tfor !shouldStop {\n   146\t\t\tselect {\n   147\t\t\tcase maxIndex := <-s.stopCh:\n   148\t\t\t\t// Make a best effort to replicate up to this index\n   149\t\t\t\tif maxIndex > 0 {\n   150\t\t\t\t\tr.replicateTo(s, maxIndex)\n   151\t\t\t\t}\n   152\t\t\t\treturn\n   153\t\t\tcase deferErr := <-s.triggerDeferErrorCh:\n   154\t\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n   155\t\t\t\tshouldStop = r.replicateTo(s, lastLogIdx)\n   156\t\t\t\tif !shouldStop {\n   157\t\t\t\t\tdeferErr.respond(nil)\n   158\t\t\t\t} else {\n   159\t\t\t\t\tdeferErr.respond(fmt.Errorf(\"replication failed\"))\n   160\t\t\t\t}\n   161\t\t\tcase <-s.triggerCh:\n   162\t\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n   163\t\t\t\tshouldStop = r.replicateTo(s, lastLogIdx)\n   164\t\t\t// This is _not_ our heartbeat mechanism but is to ensure\n   165\t\t\t// followers quickly learn the leader's commit index when\n   166\t\t\t// raft commits stop flowing naturally. The actual heartbeats\n   167\t\t\t// can't do this to keep them unblocked by disk IO on the\n   168\t\t\t// follower. See https://github.com/hashicorp/raft/issues/282.\n   169\t\t\tcase <-randomTimeout(r.config().CommitTimeout):\n   170\t\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n   171\t\t\t\tshouldStop = r.replicateTo(s, lastLogIdx)\n   172\t\t\t}\n   173\t\n   174\t\t\t// If things looks healthy, switch to pipeline mode\n   175\t\t\tif !shouldStop && s.allowPipeline {\n   176\t\t\t\tgoto PIPELINE\n   177\t\t\t}\n   178\t\t}\n   179\t\treturn\n   180\t\n   181\tPIPELINE:\n   182\t\t// Disable until re-enabled\n   183\t\ts.allowPipeline = false\n   184\t\n   185\t\t// Replicates using a pipeline for high performance. This method\n   186\t\t// is not able to gracefully recover from errors, and so we fall back\n   187\t\t// to standard mode on failure.\n   188\t\tif err := r.pipelineReplicate(s); err != nil {\n   189\t\t\tif err != ErrPipelineReplicationNotSupported {\n   190\t\t\t\ts.peerLock.RLock()\n   191\t\t\t\tpeer := s.peer\n   192\t\t\t\ts.peerLock.RUnlock()\n   193\t\t\t\tr.logger.Error(\"failed to start pipeline replication to\", \"peer\", peer, \"error\", err)\n   194\t\t\t}\n   195\t\t}\n   196\t\tgoto RPC\n   197\t}\n   198\t\n   440\t}\n   441\t\n   442\t// pipelineReplicate is used when we have synchronized our state with the follower,\n   443\t// and want to switch to a higher performance pipeline mode of replication.\n   444\t// We only pipeline AppendEntries commands, and if we ever hit an error, we fall\n   445\t// back to the standard replication which can handle more complex situations.\n   446\tfunc (r *Raft) pipelineReplicate(s *followerReplication) error {\n   447\t\ts.peerLock.RLock()\n   448\t\tpeer := s.peer\n   449\t\ts.peerLock.RUnlock()\n   450\t\n   451\t\t// Create a new pipeline\n   452\t\tpipeline, err := r.trans.AppendEntriesPipeline(peer.ID, peer.Address)\n   453\t\tif err != nil {\n   454\t\t\treturn err\n   455\t\t}\n   456\t\tdefer pipeline.Close()\n   457\t\n   458\t\t// Log start and stop of pipeline\n   459\t\tr.logger.Info(\"pipelining replication\", \"peer\", peer)\n   460\t\tdefer r.logger.Info(\"aborting pipeline replication\", \"peer\", peer)\n   461\t\n   462\t\t// Create a shutdown and finish channel\n   463\t\tstopCh := make(chan struct{})\n   464\t\tfinishCh := make(chan struct{})\n   465\t\n   466\t\t// Start a dedicated decoder\n   467\t\tr.goFunc(func() { r.pipelineDecode(s, pipeline, stopCh, finishCh) })\n   468\t\n   469\t\t// Start pipeline sends at the last good nextIndex\n   470\t\tnextIndex := atomic.LoadUint64(&s.nextIndex)\n   471\t\n   472\t\tshouldStop := false\n   473\tSEND:\n   474\t\tfor !shouldStop {\n   475\t\t\tselect {\n   476\t\t\tcase <-finishCh:\n   477\t\t\t\tbreak SEND\n   478\t\t\tcase maxIndex := <-s.stopCh:\n   479\t\t\t\t// Make a best effort to replicate up to this index\n   480\t\t\t\tif maxIndex > 0 {\n   481\t\t\t\t\tr.pipelineSend(s, pipeline, &nextIndex, maxIndex)\n   482\t\t\t\t}\n   483\t\t\t\tbreak SEND\n   484\t\t\tcase deferErr := <-s.triggerDeferErrorCh:\n   485\t\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n   486\t\t\t\tshouldStop = r.pipelineSend(s, pipeline, &nextIndex, lastLogIdx)\n   487\t\t\t\tif !shouldStop {\n   488\t\t\t\t\tdeferErr.respond(nil)\n   489\t\t\t\t} else {\n   490\t\t\t\t\tdeferErr.respond(fmt.Errorf(\"replication failed\"))\n   491\t\t\t\t}\n   492\t\t\tcase <-s.triggerCh:\n   493\t\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n   494\t\t\t\tshouldStop = r.pipelineSend(s, pipeline, &nextIndex, lastLogIdx)\n   495\t\t\tcase <-randomTimeout(r.config().CommitTimeout):\n   496\t\t\t\tlastLogIdx, _ := r.getLastLog()\n   497\t\t\t\tshouldStop = r.pipelineSend(s, pipeline, &nextIndex, lastLogIdx)\n   498\t\t\t}\n   499\t\t}\n   500\t\n   501\t\t// Stop our decoder, and wait for it to finish\n   502\t\tclose(stopCh)\n   503\t\tselect {\n   504\t\tcase <-finishCh:\n   505\t\tcase <-r.shutdownCh:\n   506\t\t}\n   507\t\treturn nil\n   508\t}\n   509\t\n   510\t// pipelineSend is used to send data over a pipeline. It is a helper to\n   511\t// pipelineReplicate.\n   512\tfunc (r *Raft) pipelineSend(s *followerReplication, p AppendPipeline, nextIdx *uint64, lastIndex uint64) (shouldStop bool) {\n   513\t\t// Create a new append request\n   514\t\treq := new(AppendEntriesRequest)\n   515\t\tif err := r.setupAppendEntries(s, req, *nextIdx, lastIndex); err != nil {\n   516\t\t\treturn true\n   517\t\t}\n   518\t\n   519\t\t// Pipeline the append entries\n   520\t\tif _, err := p.AppendEntries(req, new(AppendEntriesResponse)); err != nil {\n   521\t\t\tr.logger.Error(\"failed to pipeline appendEntries\", \"peer\", s.peer, \"error\", err)\n   522\t\t\treturn true\n   523\t\t}\n   524\t\n   525\t\t// Increase the next send log to avoid re-sending old logs\n   526\t\tif n := len(req.Entries); n > 0 {\n   527\t\t\tlast := req.Entries[n-1]\n   528\t\t\tatomic.StoreUint64(nextIdx, last.Index+1)\n   529\t\t}\n   530\t\treturn false\n   531\t}\n   532\t\n  1690\t\t// Ensure all the logs are the same\n  1691\t\tc.EnsureSame(t)\n  1692\t}\n  1693\t\n  1694\tfunc TestRaft_ReJoinFollower(t *testing.T) {\n  1695\t\t// Enable operation after a remove.\n  1696\t\tconf := inmemConfig(t)\n  1697\t\tconf.ShutdownOnRemove = false\n  1698\t\tc := MakeCluster(3, t, conf)\n  1699\t\tdefer c.Close()\n  1700\t\n  1701\t\t// Get the leader.\n  1702\t\tleader := c.Leader()\n  1703\t\n  1704\t\t// Wait until we have 2 followers.\n  1705\t\tlimit := time.Now().Add(c.longstopTimeout)\n  1706\t\tvar followers []*Raft\n  1707\t\tfor time.Now().Before(limit) && len(followers) != 2 {\n  1708\t\t\tc.WaitEvent(nil, c.conf.CommitTimeout)\n  1709\t\t\tfollowers = c.GetInState(Follower)\n  1710\t\t}\n  1711\t\tif len(followers) != 2 {\n  1712\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected two followers: %v\", followers)\n  1713\t\t}\n  1714\t\n  1715\t\t// Remove a follower.\n  1716\t\tfollower := followers[0]\n  1717\t\tfuture := leader.RemoveServer(follower.localID, 0, 0)\n  1718\t\tif err := future.Error(); err != nil {\n  1719\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"err: %v\", err)\n  1720\t\t}\n  1721\t\n  1722\t\t// Other nodes should have fewer peers.\n  1723\t\ttime.Sleep(c.propagateTimeout)\n  1724\t\tif configuration := c.getConfiguration(leader); len(configuration.Servers) != 2 {\n  1725\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"too many peers: %v\", configuration)\n  1726\t\t}\n  1727\t\tif configuration := c.getConfiguration(followers[1]); len(configuration.Servers) != 2 {\n  1728\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"too many peers: %v\", configuration)\n  1729\t\t}\n  1730\t\n  1731\t\t// Get the leader. We can't use the normal stability checker here because\n  1732\t\t// the removed server will be trying to run an election but will be\n  1733\t\t// ignored. The stability check will think this is off nominal because\n  1734\t\t// the RequestVote RPCs won't stop firing.\n  1735\t\tlimit = time.Now().Add(c.longstopTimeout)\n  1736\t\tvar leaders []*Raft\n  1737\t\tfor time.Now().Before(limit) && len(leaders) != 1 {\n  1738\t\t\tc.WaitEvent(nil, c.conf.CommitTimeout)\n  1739\t\t\tleaders, _ = c.pollState(Leader)\n  1740\t\t}\n  1741\t\tif len(leaders) != 1 {\n  1742\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected a leader\")\n  1743\t\t}\n  1744\t\tleader = leaders[0]\n  1745\t\n  1746\t\t// Rejoin. The follower will have a higher term than the leader,\n  1747\t\t// this will cause the leader to step down, and a new round of elections\n  1748\t\t// to take place. We should eventually re-stabilize.\n  1749\t\tfuture = leader.AddVoter(follower.localID, follower.localAddr, 0, 0)\n  1750\t\tif err := future.Error(); err != nil && err != ErrLeadershipLost {\n  1751\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"err: %v\", err)\n  1752\t\t}\n  1753\t\n  1754\t\t// We should level back up to the proper number of peers. We add a\n  1755\t\t// stability check here to make sure the cluster gets to a state where\n  1756\t\t// there's a solid leader.\n  1757\t\tleader = c.Leader()\n  1758\t\tif configuration := c.getConfiguration(leader); len(configuration.Servers) != 3 {\n  1759\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"missing peers: %v\", configuration)\n  1760\t\t}\n  1761\t\tif configuration := c.getConfiguration(followers[1]); len(configuration.Servers) != 3 {\n  1762\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"missing peers: %v\", configuration)\n  1763\t\t}\n  1764\t\n  1765\t\t// Should be a follower now.\n  1766\t\tif follower.State() != Follower {\n  1767\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"bad state: %v\", follower.State())\n  1768\t\t}\n  1769\t}\n  1770\t\n  1771\tfunc TestRaft_LeaderLeaseExpire(t *testing.T) {\n  1772\t\t// Make a cluster\n  1773\t\tconf := inmemConfig(t)\n  1774\t\tc := MakeCluster(2, t, conf)\n  1775\t\tdefer c.Close()\n  1776\t\n  1777\t\t// Get the leader\n  1778\t\tleader := c.Leader()\n  1779\t\n  1780\t\t// Wait until we have a followers\n  1781\t\tlimit := time.Now().Add(c.longstopTimeout)\n  1782\t\tvar followers []*Raft\n  1783\t\tfor time.Now().Before(limit) && len(followers) != 1 {\n  1784\t\t\tc.WaitEvent(nil, c.conf.CommitTimeout)\n  1785\t\t\tfollowers = c.GetInState(Follower)\n  1786\t\t}\n  1787\t\tif len(followers) != 1 {\n  1788\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected a followers: %v\", followers)\n  1789\t\t}\n  1790\t\n  1791\t\t// Disconnect the follower now\n  1792\t\tfollower := followers[0]\n  1793\t\tt.Logf(\"[INFO] Disconnecting %v\", follower)\n  1794\t\tc.Disconnect(follower.localAddr)\n  1795\t\n  1796\t\t// Watch the leaderCh\n  1797\t\ttimeout := time.After(conf.LeaderLeaseTimeout * 2)\n  1798\tLOOP:\n  1799\t\tfor {\n  1800\t\t\tselect {\n  1801\t\t\tcase v := <-leader.LeaderCh():\n  1802\t\t\t\tif !v {\n  1803\t\t\t\t\tbreak LOOP\n  1804\t\t\t\t}\n  1805\t\t\tcase <-timeout:\n  1806\t\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"timeout stepping down as leader\")\n  1807\t\t\t}\n  1808\t\t}\n  1809\t\n  1810\t\t// Ensure the last contact of the leader is non-zero\n  1811\t\tif leader.LastContact().IsZero() {\n  1812\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected non-zero contact time\")\n  1813\t\t}\n  1814\t\n  1815\t\t// Should be no leaders\n  1816\t\tif len(c.GetInState(Leader)) != 0 {\n  1817\t\t\tt.Fatalf(\"expected step down\")\n  1818\t\t}\n  1819\t\n  1820\t\t// Verify no further contact\n     1\t// Copyright (c) HashiCorp, Inc.\n     2\t// SPDX-License-Identifier: MPL-2.0\n     3\t\n     4\tpackage raft\n     5\t\n     6\timport (\n     7\t\t\"io\"\n     8\t\t\"time\"\n     9\t)\n    10\t\n    11\t// RPCResponse captures both a response and a potential error.\n    12\ttype RPCResponse struct {\n    13\t\tResponse interface{}\n    14\t\tError    error\n    15\t}\n    16\t\n    17\t// RPC has a command, and provides a response mechanism.\n    18\ttype RPC struct {\n    19\t\tCommand  interface{}\n    20\t\tReader   io.Reader // Set only for InstallSnapshot\n    21\t\tRespChan chan<- RPCResponse\n    22\t}\n    23\t\n    24\t// Respond is used to respond with a response, error or both\n    25\tfunc (r *RPC) Respond(resp interface{}, err error) {\n    26\t\tr.RespChan <- RPCResponse{resp, err}\n    27\t}\n    28\t\n    29\t// Transport provides an interface for network transports\n    30\t// to allow Raft to communicate with other nodes.\n    31\ttype Transport interface {\n    32\t\t// Consumer returns a channel that can be used to\n    33\t\t// consume and respond to RPC requests.\n    34\t\tConsumer() <-chan RPC\n    35\t\n    36\t\t// LocalAddr is used to return our local address to distinguish from our peers.\n    37\t\tLocalAddr() ServerAddress\n    38\t\n    39\t\t// AppendEntriesPipeline returns an interface that can be used to pipeline\n    40\t\t// AppendEntries requests.\n    41\t\tAppendEntriesPipeline(id ServerID, target ServerAddress) (AppendPipeline, error)\n    42\t\n    43\t\t// AppendEntries sends the appropriate RPC to the target node.\n    44\t\tAppendEntries(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *AppendEntriesRequest, resp *AppendEntriesResponse) error\n    45\t\n    46\t\t// RequestVote sends the appropriate RPC to the target node.\n    47\t\tRequestVote(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *RequestVoteRequest, resp *RequestVoteResponse) error\n    48\t\n    49\t\t// InstallSnapshot is used to push a snapshot down to a follower. The data is read from\n    50\t\t// the ReadCloser and streamed to the client.\n    51\t\tInstallSnapshot(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *InstallSnapshotRequest, resp *InstallSnapshotResponse, data io.Reader) error\n    52\t\n    53\t\t// EncodePeer is used to serialize a peer's address.\n    54\t\tEncodePeer(id ServerID, addr ServerAddress) []byte\n    55\t\n    56\t\t// DecodePeer is used to deserialize a peer's address.\n    57\t\tDecodePeer([]byte) ServerAddress\n    58\t\n    59\t\t// SetHeartbeatHandler is used to setup a heartbeat handler\n    60\t\t// as a fast-pass. This is to avoid head-of-line blocking from\n    61\t\t// disk IO. If a Transport does not support this, it can simply\n    62\t\t// ignore the call, and push the heartbeat onto the Consumer channel.\n    63\t\tSetHeartbeatHandler(cb func(rpc RPC))\n    64\t\n    65\t\t// TimeoutNow is used to start a leadership transfer to the target node.\n    66\t\tTimeoutNow(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *TimeoutNowRequest, resp *TimeoutNowResponse) error\n    67\t}\n    68\t\n    69\t// WithPreVote is an interface that a transport may provide which\n    70\t// allows a transport to support a PreVote request.\n    71\t//\n    72\t// It is defined separately from Transport as unfortunately it wasn't in the\n    73\t// original interface specification.\n    74\ttype WithPreVote interface {\n    75\t\t// RequestPreVote sends the appropriate RPC to the target node.\n    76\t\tRequestPreVote(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *RequestPreVoteRequest, resp *RequestPreVoteResponse) error\n    77\t}\n    78\t\n    79\t// WithClose is an interface that a transport may provide which\n    80\t// allows a transport to be shut down cleanly when a Raft instance\n    81\t// shuts down.\n    82\t//\n    83\t// It is defined separately from Transport as unfortunately it wasn't in the\n    84\t// original interface specification.\n    85\ttype WithClose interface {\n    86\t\t// Close permanently closes a transport, stopping\n    87\t\t// any associated goroutines and freeing other resources.\n    88\t\tClose() error\n    89\t}\n    90\t\n    91\t// LoopbackTransport is an interface that provides a loopback transport suitable for testing\n    92\t// e.g. InmemTransport. It's there so we don't have to rewrite tests.\n    93\ttype LoopbackTransport interface {\n    94\t\tTransport   // Embedded transport reference\n    95\t\tWithPeers   // Embedded peer management\n    96\t\tWithClose   // with a close routine\n    97\t\tWithPreVote // with a prevote\n    98\t}\n    99\t\n   100\t// WithPeers is an interface that a transport may provide which allows for connection and\n   101\t// disconnection. Unless the transport is a loopback transport, the transport specified to\n   102\t// \"Connect\" is likely to be nil.\n   103\ttype WithPeers interface {\n   104\t\tConnect(peer ServerAddress, t Transport) // Connect a peer\n   105\t\tDisconnect(peer ServerAddress)           // Disconnect a given peer\n   106\t\tDisconnectAll()                          // Disconnect all peers, possibly to reconnect them later\n   107\t}\n   108\t\n   109\t// AppendPipeline is used for pipelining AppendEntries requests. It is used\n   110\t// to increase the replication throughput by masking latency and better\n   111\t// utilizing bandwidth.\n   112\ttype AppendPipeline interface {\n   113\t\t// AppendEntries is used to add another request to the pipeline.\n   114\t\t// The send may block which is an effective form of back-pressure.\n   115\t\tAppendEntries(args *AppendEntriesRequest, resp *AppendEntriesResponse) (AppendFuture, error)\n   116\t\n   117\t\t// Consumer returns a channel that can be used to consume\n   118\t\t// response futures when they are ready.\n   119\t\tConsumer() <-chan AppendFuture\n   120\t\n   121\t\t// Close closes the pipeline and cancels all inflight RPCs\n   122\t\tClose() error\n   123\t}\n   124\t\n   125\t// AppendFuture is used to return information about a pipelined AppendEntries request.\n   115\t// RequestVote implements the Transport interface.\n   116\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) RequestVote(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *RequestVoteRequest, resp *RequestVoteResponse) error {\n   117\t\trpcResp, err := i.makeRPC(target, args, nil, i.timeout)\n   118\t\tif err != nil {\n   119\t\t\treturn err\n   120\t\t}\n   121\t\n   122\t\t// Copy the result back\n   123\t\tout := rpcResp.Response.(*RequestVoteResponse)\n   124\t\t*resp = *out\n   125\t\treturn nil\n   126\t}\n   127\t\n   128\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) RequestPreVote(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *RequestPreVoteRequest, resp *RequestPreVoteResponse) error {\n   129\t\trpcResp, err := i.makeRPC(target, args, nil, i.timeout)\n   130\t\tif err != nil {\n   131\t\t\treturn err\n   132\t\t}\n   133\t\n   134\t\t// Copy the result back\n   135\t\tout := rpcResp.Response.(*RequestPreVoteResponse)\n   136\t\t*resp = *out\n   137\t\treturn nil\n   138\t}\n   139\t\n   140\t// InstallSnapshot implements the Transport interface.\n   141\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) InstallSnapshot(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *InstallSnapshotRequest, resp *InstallSnapshotResponse, data io.Reader) error {\n   142\t\trpcResp, err := i.makeRPC(target, args, data, 10*i.timeout)\n   143\t\tif err != nil {\n   144\t\t\treturn err\n   145\t\t}\n   146\t\n   147\t\t// Copy the result back\n   148\t\tout := rpcResp.Response.(*InstallSnapshotResponse)\n   149\t\t*resp = *out\n   150\t\treturn nil\n   151\t}\n   152\t\n   153\t// TimeoutNow implements the Transport interface.\n   154\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) TimeoutNow(id ServerID, target ServerAddress, args *TimeoutNowRequest, resp *TimeoutNowResponse) error {\n   155\t\trpcResp, err := i.makeRPC(target, args, nil, 10*i.timeout)\n   156\t\tif err != nil {\n   157\t\t\treturn err\n   158\t\t}\n   159\t\n   160\t\t// Copy the result back\n   161\t\tout := rpcResp.Response.(*TimeoutNowResponse)\n   162\t\t*resp = *out\n   163\t\treturn nil\n   164\t}\n   165\t\n   166\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) makeRPC(target ServerAddress, args interface{}, r io.Reader, timeout time.Duration) (rpcResp RPCResponse, err error) {\n   167\t\ti.RLock()\n   168\t\tpeer, ok := i.peers[target]\n   169\t\ti.RUnlock()\n   170\t\n   171\t\tif !ok {\n   172\t\t\terr = fmt.Errorf(\"failed to connect to peer: %v\", target)\n   173\t\t\treturn\n   174\t\t}\n   175\t\n   176\t\t// Send the RPC over\n   177\t\trespCh := make(chan RPCResponse, 1)\n   178\t\treq := RPC{\n   179\t\t\tCommand:  args,\n   180\t\t\tReader:   r,\n   181\t\t\tRespChan: respCh,\n   182\t\t}\n   183\t\tselect {\n   184\t\tcase peer.consumerCh <- req:\n   185\t\tcase <-time.After(timeout):\n   186\t\t\terr = fmt.Errorf(\"send timed out\")\n   187\t\t\treturn\n   188\t\t}\n   189\t\n   190\t\t// Wait for a response\n   191\t\tselect {\n   192\t\tcase rpcResp = <-respCh:\n   193\t\t\tif rpcResp.Error != nil {\n   194\t\t\t\terr = rpcResp.Error\n   195\t\t\t}\n   196\t\tcase <-time.After(timeout):\n   197\t\t\terr = fmt.Errorf(\"command timed out\")\n   198\t\t}\n   199\t\treturn\n   200\t}\n   201\t\n   202\t// EncodePeer implements the Transport interface.\n   203\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) EncodePeer(id ServerID, p ServerAddress) []byte {\n   204\t\treturn []byte(p)\n   205\t}\n   206\t\n   207\t// DecodePeer implements the Transport interface.\n   208\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) DecodePeer(buf []byte) ServerAddress {\n   209\t\treturn ServerAddress(buf)\n   210\t}\n   211\t\n   212\t// Connect is used to connect this transport to another transport for\n   213\t// a given peer name. This allows for local routing.\n   214\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) Connect(peer ServerAddress, t Transport) {\n   215\t\ttrans := t.(*InmemTransport)\n   216\t\ti.Lock()\n   217\t\tdefer i.Unlock()\n   218\t\ti.peers[peer] = trans\n   219\t}\n   220\t\n   221\t// Disconnect is used to remove the ability to route to a given peer.\n   222\tfunc (i *InmemTransport) Disconnect(peer ServerAddress) {\n   223\t\ti.Lock()\n   224\t\tdefer i.Unlock()\n   225\t\tdelete(i.peers, peer)\n   226\t\n   227\t\t// Disconnect any pipelines\n   228\t\tn := len(i.pipelines)\n   229\t\tfor idx := 0; idx < n; idx++ {\n   230\t\t\tif i.pipelines[idx].peerAddr == peer {\n    60\t\n    61\traft is based on [\"Raft: In Search of an Understandable Consensus Algorithm\"](https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf)\n    62\t\n    63\tA high level overview of the Raft protocol is described below, but for details please read the full\n    64\t[Raft paper](https://raft.github.io/raft.pdf)\n    65\tfollowed by the raft source. Any questions about the raft protocol should be sent to the\n    66\t[raft-dev mailing list](https://groups.google.com/forum/#!forum/raft-dev).\n    67\t\n    68\t### Protocol Description\n    69\t\n    70\tRaft nodes are always in one of three states: follower, candidate or leader. All\n    71\tnodes initially start out as a follower. In this state, nodes can accept log entries\n    72\tfrom a leader and cast votes. If no entries are received for some time, nodes\n    73\tself-promote to the candidate state. In the candidate state nodes request votes from\n    74\ttheir peers. If a candidate receives a quorum of votes, then it is promoted to a leader.\n    75\tThe leader must accept new log entries and replicate to all the other followers.\n    76\tIn addition, if stale reads are not acceptable, all queries must also be performed on\n    77\tthe leader.\n    78\t\n    79\tOnce a cluster has a leader, it is able to accept new log entries. A client can\n    80\trequest that a leader append a new log entry, which is an opaque binary blob to\n    81\tRaft. The leader then writes the entry to durable storage and attempts to replicate\n    82\tto a quorum of followers. Once the log entry is considered *committed*, it can be\n    83\t*applied* to a finite state machine. The finite state machine is application specific,\n    84\tand is implemented using an interface.\n    85\t\n    86\tAn obvious question relates to the unbounded nature of a replicated log. Raft provides\n    87\ta mechanism by which the current state is snapshotted, and the log is compacted. Because\n    88\tof the FSM abstraction, restoring the state of the FSM must result in the same state\n    89\tas a replay of old logs. This allows Raft to capture the FSM state at a point in time,\n    90\tand then remove all the logs that were used to reach that state. This is performed automatically\n    91\twithout user intervention, and prevents unbounded disk usage as well as minimizing\n    92\ttime spent replaying logs.\n    93\t\n    94\tLastly, there is the issue of updating the peer set when new servers are joining\n    95\tor existing servers are leaving. As long as a quorum of nodes is available, this\n    96\tis not an issue as Raft provides mechanisms to dynamically update the peer set.\n    97\tIf a quorum of nodes is unavailable, then this becomes a very challenging issue.\n    98\tFor example, suppose there are only 2 peers, A and B. The quorum size is also\n    99\t2, meaning both nodes must agree to commit a log entry. If either A or B fails,\n   100\tit is now impossible to reach quorum. This means the cluster is unable to add,\n   101\tor remove a node, or commit any additional log entries. This results in *unavailability*.\n   102\tAt this point, manual intervention would be required to remove either A or B,\n   103\tand to restart the remaining node in bootstrap mode.\n   104\t\n   105\tA Raft cluster of 3 nodes can tolerate a single node failure, while a cluster\n   106\tof 5 can tolerate 2 node failures. The recommended configuration is to either\n   107\trun 3 or 5 raft servers. This maximizes availability without\n   108\tgreatly sacrificing performance.\n   109\t\n   110\tIn terms of performance, Raft is comparable to Paxos. Assuming stable leadership,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
